{"y":2016,"label":"2015–16","coach":"Kevin Ollie","conf":"AAC","confShort":"AAC","w":25,"l":11,"cw":11,"cl":7,"confFinish":"6th in AAC","finish":"r32","seed":9,"region":"South","apPre":20,"apHigh":18,"srs":15.89,"sos":6.28,"ortg":109.0,"drtg":94.6,"pace":66.1,"ppg":73.1,"oppg":63.5,"story":{"headline":"Jalen Adams and the Four-Overtime Miracle","text":"Ranked No. 20 in the preseason, UConn beat Michigan in the Bahamas before close losses to Syracuse (79–76) and Gonzaga (73–70) at the Battle 4 Atlantis, then bounced in and out of the polls and finished sixth in the AAC at 11–7, earning a No. 5 seed in the conference tournament. Then came one of the most memorable nights in program history: down three with 0.8 seconds left in the third overtime against Cincinnati, freshman Jalen Adams banked in a three-quarter-court shot to force a fourth overtime, and UConn won 104–97. The Huskies beat top-seeded Temple and Memphis to win the AAC tournament, with Daniel Hamilton — who averaged 12.5 points and 8.9 rebounds, second in the AAC — named MVP. As a No. 9 seed they beat Colorado 74–67 before top overall seed Kansas won 73–61. UConn finished 25–11.","moments":[{"date":"2016-03-11","title":"Jalen Adams' miracle","text":"Jalen Adams' three-quarter-court shot forces a fourth overtime; UConn beats Cincinnati 104–97."},{"date":"2016-03-13","title":"AAC Tournament Championship: W vs Memphis","text":"UConn beat Memphis 72–58 (Championship) at Amway Center, Orlando, FL."},{"date":"2016-03-17","title":"NCAA First Round: W vs Colorado","text":"UConn beat Colorado 74–67 in the NCAA tournament first round at Wells Fargo Arena, Des Moines, IA."},{"date":"2016-03-19","title":"NCAA Second Round: L vs Kansas","text":"UConn lost to Kansas 61–73 in the NCAA tournament second round at Wells Fargo Arena, Des Moines, IA."}],"honors":["American Athletic tournament champions","Daniel Hamilton – AAC Tournament MVP"],"sources":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2015%E2%80%9316_UConn_Huskies_men's_basket